                       GSL Release Checklist

This replaces the "Pre-release checks", "Notes on making a release" and
"Notes on updating documentation" sections of HACKING.  Most of the
checks are automated by scripts/prerelease.py (step 3).

X.Y below is the new version, e.g. 2.9; the git tag is release-X-Y.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Tools
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  autoconf, automake, libtool, a C and C++ compiler
  Sphinx with the RTD theme:    pip install -U Sphinx sphinx_rtd_theme
  LaTeX with latexmk (PDF manual), makeinfo (info manual)
  valgrind, abidiff (Debian/Ubuntu package abigail-tools)
  For the 32-bit x86 build:     gcc-i686-linux-gnu
  For the QEMU runs:            qemu-user-static and the cross compilers
    gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu gcc-powerpc64le-linux-gnu gcc-powerpc64-linux-gnu
    gcc-sparc64-linux-gnu gcc-arm-linux-gnueabihf gcc-riscv64-linux-gnu
    gcc-s390x-linux-gnu gcc-mips64el-linux-gnuabi64 gcc-i686-linux-gnu

  Do not install gcc-multilib: on Debian and Ubuntu it conflicts with
  the cross compilers, and installing one removes the other (the 32-bit
  build uses gcc-i686-linux-gnu instead of gcc -m32).  Mark the cross
  compilers as manually installed so that an autoremove cannot drop them:

    sudo apt-mark manual gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu ...

prerelease.py skips any check whose tools are missing and says so in
its summary; a release needs all of them to have run.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Prepare the release commit
----------------------------------------------------------------------

* configure.ac:
  - AC_INIT([gsl],[X.Y])  (X.Y+ during development; X.Y.90 etc. for a
    test release)
  - add the line "dnl gsl-X.Y  libgsl C:R:A  libgslcblas C:R:A" to the
    table of releases
  - GSL_CURRENT, GSL_REVISION, GSL_AGE (and CBLAS_*), following the
    comments there and the Libtool manual ("Updating library version
    information"), relative to the previous release:
      interfaces added only:          C+1 : 0 : A+1
      any interface removed/changed:  C+1 : 0 : 0
      no interface change:            C   : R+1 : A
    An interface "changes" also when a public struct changes in an
    incompatible way (members removed, retyped or moved, or a struct
    which users allocate themselves grows).  The abi check of
    prerelease.py lists added, removed and changed interfaces and says
    whether the numbers are consistent; read its abidiff report
    (abidiff-libgsl.so.txt) before deciding.

* NEWS: the first line is "* What is new in gsl-X.Y:"; every bug fix
  ends with "(reported by A. Lastname)" where known.

* doc/conf.py: release = 'X.Y' and the copyright years.

* doc/gsl-ref.info is distributed and installed from the repository, so
  regenerate and commit it:

    cd doc && make info

* Commit everything.  prerelease.py tests the committed revision, not
  the working tree (it warns about uncommitted changes).

----------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Run the pre-release checks
----------------------------------------------------------------------

    scripts/prerelease.py -j8 --nice 10 --workdir ~/gsl-prerelease

Run it from a normal terminal: the 32-bit x86 tests run 32-bit
programs.  A full run takes a few hours.
--list shows the checks; --only, --skip and --resume select them, e.g.

    scripts/prerelease.py --list
    scripts/prerelease.py -j8 --workdir ~/gsl-prerelease --only 'qemu:*'
    scripts/prerelease.py -j8 --workdir ~/gsl-prerelease --resume

(--resume skips the checks which passed in the previous run in the same
work directory, if it was for the same commit.)

It exports the revision with git archive and builds everything out of
tree in the work directory; the working tree is not touched.  Results:

    WORKDIR/summary.txt        one line per check, then the details
    WORKDIR/logs/CHECK.log     full output of each check
    WORKDIR/abidiff-*.txt      ABI comparison with the previous release

What it covers (see the comment at the top of the script):

  - exported symbols prefixed with gsl_/GSL_ (cblas_ in libgslcblas)
  - config.h used by the library sources and not by installed headers
  - all installed headers compiled together (C and C++) and each one on
    its own (C89 -pedantic, C99 with HAVE_INLINE, C11 after <complex.h>,
    C++)
  - version numbers consistent in configure.ac, NEWS and doc/conf.py;
    autoreconf -W all
  - make check with: default flags; strict C89; CC=c99; -std=c2x; the
    library fallbacks for the C99 math functions; -O3 with FMA
    contraction; 32-bit x86; HAVE_INLINE undefined; ASan and UBSan;
    clang and icc if installed; GSL_DISABLE_DEPRECATED (library build)
  - make check with GSL_TEST_VERBOSE=1
  - the example programs and their documented output
    (scripts/test_examples.py)
  - every test program under valgrind (suppressions in
    scripts/valgrind.supp)
  - make distcheck, also with INSTALL=install-sh
  - make dist, unpack, build the library and the tests under a virtual
    memory limit (--ulimit-kb, default 256 MB; HACKING's 64 MB is too
    small for current GCC), make check (--test-ulimit-kb)
  - the HTML, PDF and info manuals build without warnings
  - libgsl and libgslcblas interfaces versus the previous release tag,
    and the libtool version numbers
  - every test program under QEMU for aarch64, ppc64le, ppc64, sparc64,
    32-bit ARM, riscv64, s390x, mips64el and i686 (big endian, unsigned
    char, long double = double, ...)

A release needs a run with no FAIL and no SKIP other than clang/icc.
Review every WARN.  Known, accepted warnings at 2.9: config-h (inline.c
files, sincos_pi.c, integration/*.c, cblas without config.h), the
compiler warnings of the strict C89 build, autoreconf's warning about
the 'info' target in doc/Makefile.am, and abi whenever interfaces were
added or changed (it then lists them; check that the libtool numbers it
reports as consistent are right, and that the changes are compatible).

If anything is changed, commit and run again (at least the affected
checks; the release commit itself should have a complete run).

----------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Tag and make the tarball
----------------------------------------------------------------------

    git tag release-X-Y
    git push origin release-X-Y

Make the tarball from a clean export of the tag, not from a working
tree:

    git archive --prefix=gsl-X.Y-src/ release-X-Y | tar -x -C /tmp
    cd /tmp/gsl-X.Y-src
    ./autogen.sh && ./configure && make -j8 && make distcheck

which leaves gsl-X.Y.tar.gz (checked by distcheck) in that directory.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Upload
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Sign and upload to ftp-upload.gnu.org (see
https://www.gnu.org/prep/maintain/maintain.html, "Automated FTP
Uploads"):

    scripts/mkrelease.sh gsl-X.Y.tar.gz       official release, /incoming/ftp
    scripts/mktestrelease.sh gsl-X.Y.tar.gz   test release, /incoming/alpha

The script makes the directive file (official releases: with the
symlink gsl-latest.tar.gz), signs the tarball and the directive with
gpg, and prints the upload commands for the three files:

    gsl-X.Y.tar.gz
    gsl-X.Y.tar.gz.sig
    gsl-X.Y.tar.gz.directive.asc

Check a few minutes later that the release appeared on
https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gsl/ (or alpha.gnu.org) and that the
signature verifies.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
6. Update the web pages and the online manual (Savannah)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The web pages are a CVS repository on Savannah:

    export CVS_RSH=ssh
    cvs -z3 -d:ext:USER@cvs.sv.gnu.org:/web/gsl co gsl

Build the manual from the release tag (step 4's export, or doc/ of a
clean checkout of release-X-Y):

    cd doc
    make clean ; make html
    rsync -av --delete --cvs-exclude _build/ /path/to/www/gsl/doc/
    make latexpdf
    cp _build/latex/gsl-ref.pdf /path/to/www/gsl/doc/latex

Then register removed and new files with CVS:

    # files deleted by rsync --delete
    cd /path/to/www/gsl
    cvs remove

    # new files: first the directories (other than CVS), then the files
    cd /path/to/www/gsl/doc/html
    find . -type d \! -name CVS -exec cvs add '{}' \;
    find . \( -type d -name CVS -prune \) -o \( -type f -exec cvs add '{}' \; \)

    # the same for the doctrees directory
    cd /path/to/www/gsl/doc/doctrees
    find . -type d \! -name CVS -exec cvs add '{}' \;
    find . \( -type d -name CVS -prune \) -o \( -type f -exec cvs add '{}' \; \)

Edit gsl.html: the version number, the release date and the link to
NEWS.  Then:

    cd /path/to/www/gsl
    cvs commit
    cvs tag release-X-Y

----------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Announce
----------------------------------------------------------------------

* Announce on info-gsl@gnu.org and gsl-announce@sourceware.org first,
  then on info-gnu@gnu.org, so that errors are caught on the smaller
  lists.  Check the version number in every announcement.  Include the
  NEWS entries, the download location, and the sha256sum and signature
  of the tarball.

* Update the entry in the GNU Free Software Directory.

* Optionally post to na-digest (na.digest@na-net.ornl.gov).

----------------------------------------------------------------------
8. After the release
----------------------------------------------------------------------

* configure.ac: AC_INIT([gsl],[X.Y+])
* NEWS: start a section for the next version, e.g. "* What is new in
  gsl-2.10:" after 2.9
* Commit and push.
